# Generator Remote Start System — Cline Rules

## Project Context

This is an Arduino/ESP32 firmware project for a LoRa-based remote generator
start system. Two LilyGo TTGO LoRa32 T3 V1.6.1 boards communicate over
915MHz LoRa radio. Read docs/spec.md and docs/summary.md before making any changes.

---

## Repository Structure

generator-controller-radio/
  server/
    server.ino        — Server board firmware
    index.h           — Web UI embedded as C string
    secrets.h         — WiFi credentials (NOT in git — do not create or modify)
    secrets.h.example — Credential template (IS in git)
  controller/
    controller.ino    — Controller board firmware
  spec.md             — Full coding specification (read this first)
  summary.md          — Hardware and architecture summary
  .clinerules         — This file
  .gitignore          — Must contain secrets.h

---

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### Never do these things

- Never add secrets.h to git or write credentials into any tracked file
- Never initialize LoRa before WiFi in server.ino setup() — this corrupts
  WiFi on the ESP32-PICO-D4 chip and is very difficult to recover from
- Never use LORA_RST as a define name — it conflicts with the board
  definition. Always use LORA_RESET for GPIO 23
- Never use StaticJsonDocument — use JsonDocument (newer ArduinoJson API)
- Never use send_P() — use send() (newer ESPAsyncWebServer API)
- Never use the old watchdog API esp_task_wdt_init(timeout, panic) —
  use esp_task_wdt_config_t struct (ESP32 core 3.x API)
- Never call readACPresent() from inside checkForUncommandedShutdown() —
  it shares a static variable with confirmGeneratorState() and will cause
  incorrect readings. Use an inline ADC read instead
- Never use em dashes (—) in Serial.println() or any C++ string literal —
  they cause string truncation compilation errors. Use plain hyphens (-)
- Never make the ACK wait blocking (no while loop waiting for LoRa in a
  web server handler) — this triggers the watchdog and crashes the server
- Never allow a start command with no timer — the generator must never run
  indefinitely. Maximum runtime is 360 minutes (6 hours)

### Always do these things

- Always read spec.md before writing or modifying any firmware
- Always include a forward declaration block at the top of each sketch —
  Arduino IDE requires functions declared before use
- Always use LORA_RESET (not LORA_RST) for GPIO 23
- Always initialize WiFi before LoRa in server.ino setup()
- Always call WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA) and WiFi.disconnect() with delay(100)
  before WiFi.begin() to clear stale state
- Always apply WiFi.config() with static IP before WiFi.begin()
- Always feed the watchdog with esp_task_wdt_reset() as the first line
  of loop() in server.ino
- Always restart mDNS after WiFi reconnects in the WiFi health watchdog
- Always use JsonDocument (not StaticJsonDocument)
- Always use send() (not send_P()) for ESPAsyncWebServer responses
- Always add a forward declaration for every function defined after setup()
  and loop()

---

## Board Configuration

Target board: LilyGo TTGO LoRa32 T3 V1.6.1
Arduino IDE board selection: "TTGO LoRa32 V2.1 (1.6.1)"
Chip: ESP32-PICO-D4
Flash: 4MB embedded

Do not suggest changing the board selection. Other selections cause upload
failures or runtime WiFi issues specific to this chip variant.

---

## Pin Assignments — Do Not Change

```
LORA_SCK    5
LORA_MISO   19
LORA_MOSI   27
LORA_SS     18
LORA_RESET  23   (NOT LORA_RST)
LORA_DIO0   26
OLED_SDA    21
OLED_SCL    22
RELAY_PIN   13   (controller only)
BATTERY_SENSE_PIN  34  (controller only)
AC_SENSE_PIN       35  (controller only)
```

---

## LoRa Settings — Do Not Change

Both boards must use identical settings:

```
Frequency:      915E6
TX Power:       17
Bandwidth:      125E3
Spreading:      8
Coding Rate:    5
```

---

## Calibration Constants — Do Not Change

These were determined by bench measurement and must not be altered:

```
DIVIDER_RATIO         0.17543   (47kΩ/10kΩ voltage divider)
VOLTAGE_CAL_OFFSET    1.0       (calibrated against multimeter)
AC_THRESHOLD          300       (peak-to-peak ADC counts)
```

---

## Network Configuration — Do Not Change

```
Static IP:   172.17.0.10
Gateway:     172.17.0.1
Subnet:      255.255.255.0
DNS:         172.17.0.1
mDNS:        generator.local
```

---

## Timing Constants — Do Not Change Without Discussion

```
ACK_TIMEOUT_MS          15000   Server waits 15s for controller STATUS
MAX_RETRIES             1       One attempt only
HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_MS    120000  2 missed heartbeats = controller offline
GENERATOR_CONFIRM_MS    15000   Max wait for power confirmation
SHUTDOWN_SUSTAINED_MS   3000    AC must be absent for 3s to confirm shutdown
SPINUP_CHECK_INTERVAL   500     How often to sample AC during startup
OLED_TIMEOUT_MS         3600000 60 minutes
WDT_TIMEOUT_MS          30000   Hardware watchdog
WIFI_CHECK_INTERVAL     30000   WiFi health check interval
WIFI_MAX_RECONNECTS     3       Reconnect attempts before reboot
SENSE_UPDATE_INTERVAL   2000    OLED sense refresh rate
HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL      60000   Controller heartbeat period
SAFETY_TIMER_GRACE      2       Extra minutes added to controller safety timer
```

---

## Message Protocol — Do Not Change

All messages are prefixed with destination address. Format:

```
CTRL:CMD:START:90       Server to controller — start with timer in minutes
CTRL:CMD:STOP           Server to controller — stop
CTRL:CMD:STATUS         Server to controller — request status

SERV:ACK:START          Relay closed (immediate)
SERV:ACK:STOP           Relay opened (immediate)
SERV:STATUS:ON:12.7     AC confirmed present, battery voltage appended
SERV:STATUS:OFF         AC confirmed absent
SERV:STATUS:ASSUMED_ON  Relay closed, no voltage sensing hardware
SERV:STATUS:ASSUMED_OFF Relay open, no voltage sensing hardware
SERV:HB:RELAY:ON:AC:ON:BAT:12.7              Heartbeat
SERV:HB:RELAY:ON:AC:ON:BAT:11.4:WARN:CRITICAL  With warning
SERV:ERR:START_FAILED         No AC within 15s of start
SERV:ERR:STOP_FAILED          AC still present 15s after stop
SERV:ERR:UNCOMMANDED_SHUTDOWN AC lost while relay closed
SERV:ERR:SAFETY_TIMEOUT       Controller safety timer expired
SERV:ERR:UNKNOWN_CMD          Unrecognized command
```

---

## /status Endpoint Response Shape

The web UI depends on this exact shape. Do not rename or remove fields:

```json
{
  "status": "running|stopped|error|unknown|pending",
  "detail": "string",
  "pending": true,
  "timerActive": true,
  "timerRemaining": 3240,
  "battery": 12.7,
  "controllerOnline": true,
  "rssi": -67,
  "uptime": 7230,
  "lastMessageAgo": 45
}
```

`lastMessageAgo` is -1 if no message received this session.

---

## Web UI Rules

- No indefinite start — every start command must include a timer value
- Maximum timer: 360 minutes (6 hours) — enforced in both UI and firmware
- Pending state: all buttons and inputs must be disabled while a command
  is in flight; a spinner banner must be visible
- Timer countdown must be restorable from server on page reload
- No em dashes in any string — use plain hyphens
- The diagnostics section must show: server uptime, time since last LoRa
  message (amber if > 120s), RSSI with quality label (good/fair/weak)
- RSSI color coding: green > -75, amber -75 to -90, red < -90

---

## Outstanding Issues to Resolve First

Before making any other changes, verify and fix these three items:

2. lastMessageMs tracking — verify these exist in server.ino:
   - Global: unsigned long lastMessageMs = 0;
   - Updated in checkForLoRaMessage() when a valid message arrives
   - Included in /status response as lastMessageAgo

3. Boot status request — verify server sends CTRL:CMD:STATUS after LoRa
   init, and controller handles CMD:STATUS by calling sendHeartbeat()

---

## Code Style

- Constants in #define at top of file — never magic numbers in logic
- Functions grouped by purpose with comment block headers
- Forward declarations immediately after includes and globals
- Serial.print() debug output retained throughout — useful for field
  diagnostics via USB when available
- Comments on any non-obvious timing or logic decisions
- Prefer clear variable names over brevity

---

## What Not to Suggest

- Do not suggest Meshtastic — raw LoRa was chosen deliberately for
  reliability and deterministic command/control
- Do not suggest moving to HTTPS — the web server is LAN-only and the
  ESP32 TLS overhead is not justified
- Do not suggest FreeRTOS tasks for the server — the non-blocking state
  machine approach was chosen specifically to avoid watchdog conflicts
  with ESPAsyncWebServer
- Do not suggest changing the board to ESP32 Dev Module — the specific
  board definition is required for correct hardware initialization
- Do not suggest adding cloud connectivity or MQTT — no cloud dependency
  is a design requirement
